NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 "List::SomeUtils - Provide the stuff missing in List::Util"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "=head1 VERSION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 "version 0.59"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 "    # import specific functions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "    use List::SomeUtils qw( any uniq );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "    if ( any {/foo/} uniq @has_duplicates ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "        # do stuff"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 "    # import everything"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 "    use List::SomeUtils ':all';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 "B<List::SomeUtils> provides some trivial but commonly needed functionality on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "lists which is not going to go into L<List::Util>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 "All of the below functions are implementable in only a couple of lines of Perl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "code. Using the functions from this module however should give slightly better"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "performance as everything is implemented in C. The pure-Perl implementation of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 "these functions only serves as a fallback in case the C portions of this module"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 "couldn't be compiled on this machine."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "=head1 WHY DOES THIS MODULE EXIST?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 "You might wonder why this module exists when we already have"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 "L<List::MoreUtils>. In fact, this module is (nearly) the same code as is found"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 "in LMU with no significant changes. However, the LMU distribution depends on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "several modules for configuration (to run the Makefile.PL) that some folks in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "the Perl community don't think are appropriate for a module high upstream in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "the CPAN river."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 "I (Dave Rolsky) don't have a strong opinion on this, but I I<do> like the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 "functions provided by LMU, and I'm tired of getting patches and PRs to remove"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 "LMU from my code."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "This distribution exists to let me use the functionality I like without having"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 "to get into tiring arguments about issues I don't really care about."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 "=head1 EXPORTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 "=head2 Default behavior"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 "Nothing by default. To import all of this module's symbols use the C<:all> tag."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 "Otherwise functions can be imported by name as usual:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000162 "    use List::SomeUtils ':all';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000164 "    use List::SomeUtils qw{ any firstidx };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000166 "Because historical changes to the API might make upgrading List::SomeUtils"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000167 "difficult for some projects, the legacy API is available via special import"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000168 "tags."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000169 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000170 "=head1 FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000171 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000172 "=head2 Junctions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000173 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000174 "=head3 I<Treatment of an empty list>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000175 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000176 "There are two schools of thought for how to evaluate a junction on an empty"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000177 "list:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000179 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000181 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000183 "Reduction to an identity (boolean)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000185 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000186 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000187 "Result is undefined (three-valued)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000188 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000189 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000190 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000191 "In the first case, the result of the junction applied to the empty list is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000192 "determined by a mathematical reduction to an identity depending on whether the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000193 "underlying comparison is \"or\" or \"and\".  Conceptually:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000194 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000195 "                    \"any are true\"      \"all are true\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000196 "                    --------------      --------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000197 "    2 elements:     A || B || 0         A && B && 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000198 "    1 element:      A || 0              A && 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000199 "    0 elements:     0                   1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000200 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000201 "In the second case, three-value logic is desired, in which a junction applied"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000202 "to an empty list returns C<undef> rather than true or false"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000203 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000204 "Junctions with a C<_u> suffix implement three-valued logic.  Those without are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000205 "boolean."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000206 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000207 "=head3 all BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000208 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000209 "=head3 all_u BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000210 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000211 "Returns a true value if all items in LIST meet the criterion given through"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000212 "BLOCK. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000213 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000214 "  print \"All values are non-negative\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000215 "    if all { $_ >= 0 } ($x, $y, $z);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000216 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000217 "For an empty LIST, C<all> returns true (i.e. no values failed the condition)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000218 "and C<all_u> returns C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000219 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000220 "Thus, C<< all_u(@list) >> is equivalent to C<< @list ? all(@list) : undef >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000221 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000222 "B<Note>: because Perl treats C<undef> as false, you must check the return value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000223 "of C<all_u> with C<defined> or you will get the opposite result of what you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000224 "expect."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000226 "=head3 any BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000228 "=head3 any_u BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000230 "Returns a true value if any item in LIST meets the criterion given through"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000231 "BLOCK. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000233 "  print \"At least one non-negative value\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000234 "    if any { $_ >= 0 } ($x, $y, $z);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000235 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000236 "For an empty LIST, C<any> returns false and C<any_u> returns C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000238 "Thus, C<< any_u(@list) >> is equivalent to C<< @list ? any(@list) : undef >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000239 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000240 "=head3 none BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000241 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000242 "=head3 none_u BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000243 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000244 "Logically the negation of C<any>. Returns a true value if no item in LIST meets"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000245 "the criterion given through BLOCK. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000246 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000247 "  print \"No non-negative values\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000248 "    if none { $_ >= 0 } ($x, $y, $z);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000250 "For an empty LIST, C<none> returns true (i.e. no values failed the condition)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000251 "and C<none_u> returns C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000252 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000253 "Thus, C<< none_u(@list) >> is equivalent to C<< @list ? none(@list) : undef >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000254 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000255 "B<Note>: because Perl treats C<undef> as false, you must check the return value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000256 "of C<none_u> with C<defined> or you will get the opposite result of what you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000257 "expect."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000259 "=head3 notall BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000260 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000261 "=head3 notall_u BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000262 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000263 "Logically the negation of C<all>. Returns a true value if not all items in LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000264 "meet the criterion given through BLOCK. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000265 "turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000266 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000267 "  print \"Not all values are non-negative\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000268 "    if notall { $_ >= 0 } ($x, $y, $z);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000270 "For an empty LIST, C<notall> returns false and C<notall_u> returns C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000272 "Thus, C<< notall_u(@list) >> is equivalent to C<< @list ? notall(@list) : undef"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000273 ">>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000274 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000275 "=head3 one BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000276 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000277 "=head3 one_u BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000278 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000279 "Returns a true value if precisely one item in LIST meets the criterion given"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000280 "through BLOCK. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000281 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000282 "    print \"Precisely one value defined\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000283 "        if one { defined($_) } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000284 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000285 "Returns false otherwise."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000287 "For an empty LIST, C<one> returns false and C<one_u> returns C<undef>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000289 "The expression C<one BLOCK LIST> is almost equivalent to C<1 == true BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000290 "LIST>, except for short-cutting. Evaluation of BLOCK will immediately stop at"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000291 "the second true value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000292 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000293 "=head2 Transformation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000294 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000295 "=head3 apply BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000296 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000297 "Makes a copy of the list and then passes each element I<from the copy> to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000298 "BLOCK. Any changes or assignments to C<$_> in the BLOCK will only affect the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000299 "elements of the new list. However, if C<$_> is a reference then changes to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000300 "referenced value will be seen in both the original and new list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000301 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000302 "This function is similar to C<map> but will not modify the elements of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000303 "input list:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000305 "  my @list = (1 .. 4);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000306 "  my @mult = apply { $_ *= 2 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000307 "  print \"\\@list = @list\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000308 "  print \"\\@mult = @mult\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000309 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000310 "  @list = 1 2 3 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000311 "  @mult = 2 4 6 8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000313 "Think of it as syntactic sugar for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000315 "  for (my @mult = @list) { $_ *= 2 }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000317 "Note that you must alter C<$_> directly inside BLOCK in order for changes to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000318 "make effect. New value returned from the BLOCK are ignored:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000319 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000320 "  # @new is identical to @list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000321 "  my @new = apply { $_ * 2 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000323 "  # @new is different from @list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000324 "  my @new = apply { $_ =* 2 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000326 "=head3 insert_after BLOCK VALUE LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000328 "Inserts VALUE after the first item in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000329 "true. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000331 "  my @list = qw/This is a list/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000332 "  insert_after { $_ eq \"a\" } \"longer\" => @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000333 "  print \"@list\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000334 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000335 "  This is a longer list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000337 "=head3 insert_after_string STRING VALUE LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000338 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000339 "Inserts VALUE after the first item in LIST which is equal to STRING."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000340 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000341 "  my @list = qw/This is a list/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000342 "  insert_after_string \"a\", \"longer\" => @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000343 "  print \"@list\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000344 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000345 "  This is a longer list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000346 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000347 "=head3 pairwise BLOCK ARRAY1 ARRAY2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000348 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000349 "Evaluates BLOCK for each pair of elements in ARRAY1 and ARRAY2 and returns a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000350 "new list consisting of BLOCK's return values. The two elements are set to C<$a>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000351 "and C<$b>.  Note that those two are aliases to the original value so changing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000352 "them will modify the input arrays."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000354 "  @a = (1 .. 5);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000355 "  @b = (11 .. 15);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000356 "  @x = pairwise { $a + $b } @a, @b;     # returns 12, 14, 16, 18, 20"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000357 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000358 "  # mesh with pairwise"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000359 "  @a = qw/a b c/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000360 "  @b = qw/1 2 3/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000361 "  @x = pairwise { ($a, $b) } @a, @b;    # returns a, 1, b, 2, c, 3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000363 "=head3 mesh ARRAY1 ARRAY2 [ ARRAY3 ... ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000364 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000365 "=head3 zip ARRAY1 ARRAY2 [ ARRAY3 ... ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000366 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000367 "Returns a list consisting of the first elements of each array, then the second,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000368 "then the third, etc, until all arrays are exhausted."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000369 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000370 "Examples:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000372 "  @x = qw/a b c d/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000373 "  @y = qw/1 2 3 4/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000374 "  @z = mesh @x, @y;         # returns a, 1, b, 2, c, 3, d, 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000376 "  @a = ('x');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000377 "  @b = ('1', '2');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000378 "  @c = qw/zip zap zot/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000379 "  @d = mesh @a, @b, @c;   # x, 1, zip, undef, 2, zap, undef, undef, zot"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000380 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000381 "C<zip> is an alias for C<mesh>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000382 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000383 "=head3 uniq LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000384 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000385 "=head3 distinct LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000387 "Returns a new list by stripping duplicate values in LIST by comparing the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000388 "values as hash keys, except that undef is considered separate from ''. The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000389 "order of elements in the returned list is the same as in LIST. In scalar"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000390 "context, returns the number of unique elements in LIST."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000391 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000392 "  my @x = uniq 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 5, 3, 4; # returns 1 2 3 5 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000393 "  my $x = uniq 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 5, 3, 4; # returns 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000394 "  # returns \"Mike\", \"Michael\", \"Richard\", \"Rick\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000395 "  my @n = distinct \"Mike\", \"Michael\", \"Richard\", \"Rick\", \"Michael\", \"Rick\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000396 "  # returns '', undef, 'S1', A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000397 "  my @s = distinct '', undef, 'S1', 'A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000398 "  # returns '', undef, 'S1', A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000399 "  my @w = uniq undef, '', 'S1', 'A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000400 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000401 "C<distinct> is an alias for C<uniq>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000402 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000403 "B<RT#49800> can be used to give feedback about this behavior."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000404 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000405 "=head3 singleton"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000406 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000407 "Returns a new list by stripping values in LIST occurring more than once by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000408 "comparing the values as hash keys, except that undef is considered separate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000409 "from ''.  The order of elements in the returned list is the same as in LIST. In"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000410 "scalar context, returns the number of elements occurring only once in LIST."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000411 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000412 "  my @x = singleton 1,1,2,2,3,4,5 # returns 3 4 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000414 "=head2 Partitioning"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000416 "=head3 after BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000418 "Returns a list of the values of LIST after (and not including) the point where"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000419 "BLOCK returns a true value. Sets C<$_> for each element in LIST in turn."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000420 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000421 "  @x = after { $_ % 5 == 0 } (1..9);    # returns 6, 7, 8, 9"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000423 "=head3 after_incl BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000424 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000425 "Same as C<after> but also includes the element for which BLOCK is true."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000426 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000427 "=head3 before BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000428 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000429 "Returns a list of values of LIST up to (and not including) the point where"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000430 "BLOCK returns a true value. Sets C<$_> for each element in LIST in turn."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000432 "=head3 before_incl BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000434 "Same as C<before> but also includes the element for which BLOCK is true."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000436 "=head3 part BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000438 "Partitions LIST based on the return value of BLOCK which denotes into which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000439 "partition the current value is put."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000441 "Returns a list of the partitions thusly created. Each partition created is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000442 "reference to an array."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000443 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000444 "  my $i = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000445 "  my @part = part { $i++ % 2 } 1 .. 8;   # returns [1, 3, 5, 7], [2, 4, 6, 8]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000446 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000447 "You can have a sparse list of partitions as well where non-set partitions will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000448 "be undef:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000450 "  my @part = part { 2 } 1 .. 10;            # returns undef, undef, [ 1 .. 10 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000451 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000452 "Be careful with negative values, though:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000454 "  my @part = part { -1 } 1 .. 10;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000455 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000456 "  Modification of non-creatable array value attempted, subscript -1 ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000457 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000458 "Negative values are only ok when they refer to a partition previously created:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000459 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000460 "  my @idx  = ( 0, 1, -1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000461 "  my $i    = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000462 "  my @part = part { $idx[$i++ % 3] } 1 .. 8; # [1, 4, 7], [2, 3, 5, 6, 8]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000464 "=head2 Iteration"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000465 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000466 "=head3 each_array ARRAY1 ARRAY2 ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000468 "Creates an array iterator to return the elements of the list of arrays ARRAY1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000469 "ARRAY2 throughout ARRAYn in turn.  That is, the first time it is called, it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000470 "returns the first element of each array.  The next time, it returns the second"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000471 "elements.  And so on, until all elements are exhausted."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000472 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000473 "This is useful for looping over more than one array at once:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000474 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000475 "  my $ea = each_array(@a, @b, @c);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000476 "  while ( my ($a, $b, $c) = $ea->() )   { .... }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000477 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000478 "The iterator returns the empty list when it reached the end of all arrays."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000480 "If the iterator is passed an argument of 'C<index>', then it returns the index"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000481 "of the last fetched set of values, as a scalar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000482 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000483 "=head3 each_arrayref LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000484 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000485 "Like each_array, but the arguments are references to arrays, not the plain"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000486 "arrays."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000487 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000488 "=head3 natatime EXPR, LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000489 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000490 "Creates an array iterator, for looping over an array in chunks of C<$n> items"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000491 "at a time.  (n at a time, get it?).  An example is probably a better"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000492 "explanation than I could give in words."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000493 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000494 "Example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000495 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000496 "  my @x = ('a' .. 'g');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000497 "  my $it = natatime 3, @x;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000498 "  while (my @vals = $it->())"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000499 "  {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000500 "    print \"@vals\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000501 "  }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000503 "This prints"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000504 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000505 "  a b c"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000506 "  d e f"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000507 "  g"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000508 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000509 "=head2 Searching"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000510 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000511 "=head3 bsearch BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000512 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000513 "Performs a binary search on LIST which must be a sorted list of values. BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000514 "must return a negative value if the current element (stored in C<$_>) is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000515 "smaller, a positive value if it is bigger and zero if it matches."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000516 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000517 "Returns a boolean value in scalar context. In list context, it returns the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000518 "element if it was found, otherwise the empty list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000519 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000520 "=head3 bsearchidx BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000521 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000522 "=head3 bsearch_index BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000523 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000524 "Performs a binary search on LIST which must be a sorted list of values. BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000525 "must return a negative value if the current element (stored in C<$_>) is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000526 "smaller, a positive value if it is bigger and zero if it matches."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000527 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000528 "Returns the index of found element, otherwise C<-1>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000529 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000530 "C<bsearch_index> is an alias for C<bsearchidx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000531 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000532 "=head3 firstval BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000533 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000534 "=head3 first_value BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000536 "Returns the first element in LIST for which BLOCK evaluates to true. Each"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000537 "element of LIST is set to C<$_> in turn. Returns C<undef> if no such element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000538 "has been found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000539 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000540 "C<first_value> is an alias for C<firstval>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000541 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000542 "=head3 onlyval BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000544 "=head3 only_value BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000545 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000546 "Returns the only element in LIST for which BLOCK evaluates to true. Sets C<$_>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000547 "for each item in LIST in turn. Returns C<undef> if no such element has been"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000548 "found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000549 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000550 "C<only_value> is an alias for C<onlyval>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000551 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000552 "=head3 lastval BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000553 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000554 "=head3 last_value BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000555 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000556 "Returns the last value in LIST for which BLOCK evaluates to true. Each element"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000557 "of LIST is set to C<$_> in turn. Returns C<undef> if no such element has been"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000558 "found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000559 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000560 "C<last_value> is an alias for C<lastval>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000561 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000562 "=head3 firstres BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000563 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000564 "=head3 first_result BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000566 "Returns the result of BLOCK for the first element in LIST for which BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000567 "evaluates to true. Each element of LIST is set to C<$_> in turn. Returns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000568 "C<undef> if no such element has been found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000569 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000570 "C<first_result> is an alias for C<firstres>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000571 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000572 "=head3 onlyres BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000574 "=head3 only_result BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000576 "Returns the result of BLOCK for the first element in LIST for which BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000577 "evaluates to true. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn. Returns C<undef>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000578 "if no such element has been found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000579 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000580 "C<only_result> is an alias for C<onlyres>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000581 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000582 "=head3 lastres BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000583 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000584 "=head3 last_result BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000585 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000586 "Returns the result of BLOCK for the last element in LIST for which BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000587 "evaluates to true. Each element of LIST is set to C<$_> in turn. Returns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000588 "C<undef> if no such element has been found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000589 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000590 "C<last_result> is an alias for C<lastres>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000591 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000592 "=head3 indexes BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000593 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000594 "Evaluates BLOCK for each element in LIST (assigned to C<$_>) and returns a list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000595 "of the indices of those elements for which BLOCK returned a true value. This is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000596 "just like C<grep> only that it returns indices instead of values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000597 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000598 "  @x = indexes { $_ % 2 == 0 } (1..10);   # returns 1, 3, 5, 7, 9"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000599 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000600 "=head3 firstidx BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000601 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000602 "=head3 first_index BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000603 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000604 "Returns the index of the first element in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000605 "is true. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000606 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000607 "  my @list = (1, 4, 3, 2, 4, 6);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000608 "  printf \"item with index %i in list is 4\", firstidx { $_ == 4 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000609 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000610 "  item with index 1 in list is 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000611 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000612 "Returns C<-1> if no such item could be found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000613 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000614 "C<first_index> is an alias for C<firstidx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000616 "=head3 onlyidx BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000618 "=head3 only_index BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000619 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000620 "Returns the index of the only element in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000621 "is true. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000623 "    my @list = (1, 3, 4, 3, 2, 4);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000624 "    printf \"unique index of item 2 in list is %i\", onlyidx { $_ == 2 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000625 "    __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000626 "    unique index of item 2 in list is 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000627 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000628 "Returns C<-1> if either no such item or more than one of these has been found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000629 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000630 "C<only_index> is an alias for C<onlyidx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000631 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000632 "=head3 lastidx BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000634 "=head3 last_index BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000635 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000636 "Returns the index of the last element in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000637 "is true. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000638 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000639 "  my @list = (1, 4, 3, 2, 4, 6);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000640 "  printf \"item with index %i in list is 4\", lastidx { $_ == 4 } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000641 "  __END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000642 "  item with index 4 in list is 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000643 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000644 "Returns C<-1> if no such item could be found."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000645 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000646 "C<last_index> is an alias for C<lastidx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000647 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000648 "=head2 Sorting"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000649 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000650 "=head3 sort_by BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000651 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000652 "Returns the list of values sorted according to the string values returned by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000653 "the KEYFUNC block or function. A typical use of this may be to sort objects"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000654 "according to the string value of some accessor, such as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000655 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000656 "  sort_by { $_->name } @people"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000657 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000658 "The key function is called in scalar context, being passed each value in turn"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000659 "as both $_ and the only argument in the parameters, @_. The values are then"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000660 "sorted according to string comparisons on the values returned. This is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000661 "equivalent to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000662 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000663 "  sort { $a->name cmp $b->name } @people"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000664 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000665 "except that it guarantees the name accessor will be executed only once per"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000666 "value. One interesting use-case is to sort strings which may have numbers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000667 "embedded in them \"naturally\", rather than lexically."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000668 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000669 "  sort_by { s/(\\d+)/sprintf \"%09d\", $1/eg; $_ } @strings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000670 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000671 "This sorts strings by generating sort keys which zero-pad the embedded numbers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000672 "to some level (9 digits in this case), helping to ensure the lexical sort puts"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000673 "them in the correct order."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000674 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000675 "=head3 nsort_by BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000676 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000677 "Similar to sort_by but compares its key values numerically."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000679 "=head2 Counting and calculation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000680 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000681 "=head3 true BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000682 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000683 "Counts the number of elements in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK is true."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000684 "Sets C<$_> for  each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000685 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000686 "  printf \"%i item(s) are defined\", true { defined($_) } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000687 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000688 "=head3 false BLOCK LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000690 "Counts the number of elements in LIST for which the criterion in BLOCK is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000691 "false. Sets C<$_> for each item in LIST in turn:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000692 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000693 "  printf \"%i item(s) are not defined\", false { defined($_) } @list;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000694 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000695 "=head3 minmax LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000696 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000697 "Calculates the minimum and maximum of LIST and returns a two element list with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000698 "the first element being the minimum and the second the maximum. Returns the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000699 "empty list if LIST was empty."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000700 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000701 "The C<minmax> algorithm differs from a naive iteration over the list where each"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000702 "element is compared to two values being the so far calculated min and max value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000703 "in that it only requires 3n/2 - 2 comparisons. Thus it is the most efficient"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000704 "possible algorithm."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000705 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000706 "However, the Perl implementation of it has some overhead simply due to the fact"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000707 "that there are more lines of Perl code involved. Therefore, LIST needs to be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000708 "fairly big in order for C<minmax> to win over a naive implementation. This"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000709 "limitation does not apply to the XS version."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000710 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000711 "=head3 mode LIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000713 "Calculates the most common items in the list and returns them as a list. This"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000714 "is effectively done by string comparisons, so references will be stringified."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000715 "If they implement string overloading, this will be used."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000716 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000717 "If more than one item appears the same number of times in the list, all such"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000718 "items will be returned. For example, the mode of a unique list is the list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000719 "itself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000720 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000721 "This function returns a list in list context. In scalar context it returns a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000722 "count indicating the number of modes in the list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000723 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000724 "=head1 MAINTENANCE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000725 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000726 "The maintenance goal is to preserve the documented semantics of the API; bug"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000727 "fixes that bring actual behavior in line with semantics are allowed. New API"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000728 "functions may be added over time.  If a backwards incompatible change is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000729 "unavoidable, we will attempt to provide support for the legacy API using the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000730 "same export tag mechanism currently in place."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000731 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000732 "This module attempts to use few non-core dependencies. Non-core configuration"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000733 "and testing modules will be bundled when reasonable; run-time dependencies will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000734 "be added only if they deliver substantial benefit."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000735 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000736 "=head1 KNOWN ISSUES"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000737 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000738 "There is a problem with a bug in 5.6.x perls. It is a syntax error to write"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000739 "things like:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000740 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000741 "    my @x = apply { s/foo/bar/ } qw{ foo bar baz };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000742 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000743 "It has to be written as either"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000744 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000745 "    my @x = apply { s/foo/bar/ } 'foo', 'bar', 'baz';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000746 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000747 "or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000748 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000749 "    my @x = apply { s/foo/bar/ } my @dummy = qw/foo bar baz/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000750 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000751 "Perl 5.5.x and Perl 5.8.x don't suffer from this limitation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000752 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000753 "If you have a functionality that you could imagine being in this module, please"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000754 "drop me a line. This module's policy will be less strict than L<List::Util>'s"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000755 "when it comes to additions as it isn't a core module."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000756 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000757 "When you report bugs, it would be nice if you could additionally give me the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000758 "output of your program with the environment variable C<LIST_MOREUTILS_PP> set"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000759 "to a true value. That way I know where to look for the problem (in XS,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000760 "pure-Perl or possibly both)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000761 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000762 "=head1 THANKS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000763 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000764 "=head2 Tassilo von Parseval"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000765 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000766 "Credits go to a number of people: Steve Purkis for giving me namespace advice"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000767 "and James Keenan and Terrence Branno for their effort of keeping the CPAN"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000768 "tidier by making L<List::Util> obsolete."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000769 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000770 "Brian McCauley suggested the inclusion of apply() and provided the pure-Perl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000771 "implementation for it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000772 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000773 "Eric J. Roode asked me to add all functions from his module C<List::SomeUtil>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000774 "into this one. With minor modifications, the pure-Perl implementations of those"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000775 "are by him."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000776 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000777 "The bunch of people who almost immediately pointed out the many problems with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000778 "the glitchy 0.07 release (Slaven Rezic, Ron Savage, CPAN testers)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000779 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000780 "A particularly nasty memory leak was spotted by Thomas A. Lowery."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000781 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000782 "Lars Thegler made me aware of problems with older Perl versions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000783 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000784 "Anno Siegel de-orphaned each_arrayref()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000785 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000786 "David Filmer made me aware of a problem in each_arrayref that could ultimately"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000787 "lead to a segfault."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000788 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000789 "Ricardo Signes suggested the inclusion of part() and provided the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000790 "Perl-implementation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000791 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000792 "Robin Huston kindly fixed a bug in perl's MULTICALL API to make the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000793 "XS-implementation of part() work."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000794 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000795 "=head2 Jens Rehsack"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000796 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000797 "Credits goes to all people contributing feedback during the v0.400 development"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000798 "releases."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000800 "Special thanks goes to David Golden who spent a lot of effort to develop a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000801 "design to support current state of CPAN as well as ancient software somewhere"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000802 "in the dark. He also contributed a lot of patches to refactor the API frontend"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000803 "to welcome any user of List::SomeUtils - from ancient past to recently last"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000804 "used."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000806 "Toby Inkster provided a lot of useful feedback for sane importer code and was a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000807 "nice sounding board for API discussions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000808 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000809 "Peter Rabbitson provided a sane git repository setup containing entire package"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000810 "history."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000811 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000812 "=head1 TODO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000813 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000814 "A pile of requests from other people is still pending further processing in my"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000815 "mailbox. This includes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000816 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000817 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000818 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000819 "=item * List::Util export pass-through"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000820 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000821 "Allow B<List::SomeUtils> to pass-through the regular L<List::Util> functions to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000822 "end users only need to C<use> the one module."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000823 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000824 "=item * uniq_by(&@)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000825 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000826 "Use code-reference to extract a key based on which the uniqueness is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000827 "determined. Suggested by Aaron Crane."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000828 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000829 "=item * delete_index"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000830 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000831 "=item * random_item"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000832 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000833 "=item * random_item_delete_index"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000834 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000835 "=item * list_diff_hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000836 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000837 "=item * list_diff_inboth"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000838 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000839 "=item * list_diff_infirst"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000840 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000841 "=item * list_diff_insecond"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000842 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000843 "These were all suggested by Dan Muey."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000844 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000845 "=item * listify"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000846 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000847 "Always return a flat list when either a simple scalar value was passed or an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000848 "array-reference. Suggested by Mark Summersault."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000849 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000850 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000851 ""
